values
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q1', 'Q1', 'Part 0', 1, 51.02, 492.02, 544.25, 647.93, 580.28, 811.89, 'Calculate', 3, 'Calculate $4 \times (5+3)$ in two different ways: (a) by evaluating the bracket first, and (b) by multiplying 4 by each term inside the bracket separately and adding the results. State whether the two answers match.', '(a) $4 \times (5+3) = 4 \times 8 = 32$. (b) $4 \times 5 + 4 \times 3 = 20 + 12 = 32$. The two answers match.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 0, 'f95ce2a1-cabb-5282-a5e7-08b140b8d271'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q2', 'Q2', null, 2, 51.02, 88.85, 544.25, 244.76, 580.28, 316.79, null, 2, 'Using the definition $a - b := a + (-b)$ from A.2, rewrite $9 - 4$ as an addition, and calculate its value.', '$9 - 4 = 9 + (-4) = 5$.', null, 2, 'auto_fillrect', 1, '03238bd0-1ed2-53b5-be77-1f46e8d4a412'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q3', 'Q3', null, 2, 51.02, 320.79, 544.25, 476.69, 580.28, 528.27, 'Justify', 3, 'State whether $7 - 3 = 3 - 7$. Justify your answer by writing both sides as additions using the definition from A.2 and comparing the results.', '$7 - 3 = 7 + (-3) = 4$ and $3 - 7 = 3 + (-7) = -4$. Since $4 \neq -4$, the statement is false: $7 - 3 \neq 3 - 7$.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 2, '79794272-aa4d-5789-8bc9-bb25d7e8dcc9'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q4', 'Q4', null, 2, 51.02, 532.27, 544.25, 688.17, 580.28, 811.89, 'Verify', 3, 'In A.1 it was shown that $60a + 30c = 30(2a + c)$ using an area-model argument. Verify this result numerically for $a = 2$ and $c = 4$ by calculating both sides.', 'Left side: $60(2) + 30(4) = 120 + 120 = 240$. Right side: $30(2 \times 2 + 4) = 30(8) = 240$. Both sides equal 240, so the result is verified for these values.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 3, '46e36d13-cbdc-5a32-90eb-2309750482ce'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q5', 'Q5', 'Part 1', 3, 51.02, 313.49, 544.25, 469.40, 580.28, 520.97, 'Investigate', 4, 'Investigate the statement $a + b = b + a$ by calculating both sides for three different pairs of numbers of your choice, including at least one negative number. State your conjecture about addition in one sentence.', 'Any three correct pairs, one involving a negative. For example $3+5 = 8$ and $5+3 = 8$; $-2+7 = 5$ and $7+(-2) = 5$; $0.5+4 = 4.5$ and $4+0.5 = 4.5$. Conjecture: adding two numbers gives the same result whichever order they are added in.', null, 4, 'auto_fillrect', 4, 'fa21ed2e-4d82-5746-ab8b-dbea96e89ff5'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q10', 'Q10', null, 3, 51.02, 524.97, 544.25, 680.88, 580.28, 811.89, 'Calculate', 3, 'Investigate whether the same swapping rule holds for subtraction. Calculate $a - b$ and $b - a$ for $a = 10$, $b = 4$, then state whether subtraction is commutative.', '$10 - 4 = 6$ and $4 - 10 = -6$. Since $6 \neq -6$, subtraction is not commutative.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 5, 'a26b4c02-fda7-527d-ac50-81d15136a8c8'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q11', 'Q11', null, 4, 51.02, 110.40, 544.25, 266.31, 580.28, 317.61, 'Investigate', 4, 'Investigate the statement $(a + b) + c = a + (b + c)$ by calculating both sides for $a = 2$, $b = 5$, $c = 9$. State your conjecture about how addition can be grouped.', 'Left side: $(2+5)+9 = 7+9 = 16$. Right side: $2+(5+9) = 2+14 = 16$. Both equal 16. Conjecture: when three numbers are added, it does not matter which two are added first.', null, 4, 'auto_fillrect', 6, '9e7532d6-9492-5ec6-a103-3d0c15433705'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q12', 'Q12', null, 4, 51.02, 321.61, 544.25, 477.52, 580.28, 543.48, 'Determine', 4, 'Determine whether $(a - b) - c$ is equal to $a - (b - c)$ for $a = 20$, $b = 8$, $c = 3$. Explain what this tells you about subtraction.', '$(20-8)-3 = 12-3 = 9$ and $20-(8-3) = 20-5 = 15$. Since $9 \neq 15$, the two are not equal, so subtraction is not associative — the grouping changes the result.', null, 4, 'auto_fillrect', 7, 'd190c9cb-b60e-54c2-b7cf-7e8ab1ea459a'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q13', 'Q13', null, 4, 51.02, 547.48, 544.25, 703.39, 580.28, 811.89, 'Compare', 4, 'Compare the commutative property $a + b = b + a$ with the associative property $(a + b) + c = a + (b + c)$. Explain, in your own words, the difference between swapping two numbers and regrouping three numbers.', 'The commutative property is about ORDER: it involves two numbers and lets them change places. The associative property is about GROUPING: it involves three numbers kept in the same order and only moves the brackets, changing which pair is combined first. Commutativity changes order without changing grouping; associativity changes grouping without changing order.', null, 4, 'auto_fillrect', 8, '4001c12d-a64a-5639-b557-b2c65719f29a'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q14', 'Q14', 'Part 2', 5, 51.02, 455.18, 544.25, 611.09, 580.28, 811.89, 'Construct', 3, 'A rectangle has width $a$ and is split along its length into two parts of length $b$ and $c$, so its total length is $b + c$. Construct a labelled diagram of this rectangle, showing the two smaller rectangles formed.', 'A single rectangle of width $a$ and total length $b+c$, divided by one vertical line into a left rectangle of dimensions $a$ by $b$ and a right rectangle of dimensions $a$ by $c$. All four of $a$, $b$, $c$ and $b+c$ labelled.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 9, 'dfcf40af-7d45-5933-8f14-57216e48d0b8'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q15', 'Q15', null, 6, 51.02, 135.90, 544.25, 291.80, 580.28, 357.22, null, 5, 'Using your diagram from the previous question, show that the total area of the large rectangle equals the sum of the areas of the two smaller rectangles, and hence show that $a(b+c) = ab + ac$.', 'The large rectangle has width $a$ and length $b+c$, so its area is $a(b+c)$. The two smaller rectangles have areas $ab$ and $ac$, so together they have area $ab + ac$. Cutting the rectangle does not change its total area, so the two expressions describe the same area, giving $a(b+c) = ab + ac$.', null, 5, 'auto_fillrect', 10, '3720c0d3-923b-5493-b09f-b2e513cf2cef'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q16', 'Q16', null, 6, 51.02, 361.22, 544.25, 517.12, 580.28, 568.15, 'Explain', 4, 'Explain why the distributive property $a(b+c) = ab+ac$ also justifies the reverse direction, $ab+ac = a(b+c)$, which is called factoring. Verify this reverse direction numerically using $a = 7$, $b = 3$, $c = 5$.', 'An equation is a statement that two expressions are the same quantity, so it may be read in either direction; proving $a(b+c) = ab+ac$ therefore also establishes $ab+ac = a(b+c)$. Verification: $7(3) + 7(5) = 21 + 35 = 56$ and $7(3+5) = 7(8) = 56$. Both sides equal 56.', null, 4, 'auto_fillrect', 11, 'cc927561-8804-521c-90b7-565f7f5f3508'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q17', 'Q17', null, 6, 51.02, 572.15, 544.25, 728.05, 580.28, 811.89, 'Investigate', 4, 'Investigate whether the distributive property extends to three terms inside the bracket: calculate both sides of $a(b+c+d) = ab+ac+ad$ for $a = 2$, $b = 3$, $c = 4$, $d = 5$. State your conjecture.', 'Left side: $2(3+4+5) = 2(12) = 24$. Right side: $2(3) + 2(4) + 2(5) = 6 + 8 + 10 = 24$. Both equal 24. Conjecture: the distributive property extends to any number of terms inside the bracket — the multiplier is applied to each term in turn.', null, 4, 'auto_fillrect', 12, '195673f5-fcab-5ae4-9c21-2fff8d476243'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q18', 'Q18', null, 7, 51.02, 87.69, 544.25, 243.60, 580.28, 260.55, 'Determine', 2, 'Determine, using the distributive property, an expanded expression equivalent to $6(2x + 5)$.', '$6(2x+5) = 6(2x) + 6(5) = 12x + 30$ by the distributive property.', null, 2, 'auto_fillrect', 13, 'edf45b5a-b97f-5082-9fa5-6bbd6f99d5fb'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q19', 'Q19', 'Part 3', 8, 51.02, 89.40, 544.25, 245.31, 580.28, 343.65, 'Verify', 2, 'Consider the two expressions $3(x + 4) + 2x$ and $5x + 12$. Verify they are equivalent by substituting $x = 6$ into both.', '$3(6+4) + 2(6) = 3(10) + 12 = 30 + 12 = 42$ and $5(6) + 12 = 30 + 12 = 42$. Both give 42, so the expressions agree at $x = 6$.', null, 2, 'auto_fillrect', 14, '32d825d0-45dc-5d9b-abc8-8d7a24f89c49'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q20', 'Q20', null, 8, 51.02, 347.65, 544.25, 503.56, 580.28, 575.87, 'Explain', 6, 'Prove that $3(x + 4) + 2x = 5x + 12$ for every value of $x$, using the distributive property and naming each property you use. Explain why this proof is stronger evidence than the single substitution in the previous question.', '$3(x+4) + 2x = 3x + 12 + 2x$ (distributive property) $= 3x + 2x + 12$ (commutative property of addition) $= (3x + 2x) + 12$ (associative property of addition) $= 5x + 12$ (distributive property in reverse, since $3x + 2x = (3+2)x$). The proof uses only properties that hold for every number, so it establishes the equality for every value of $x$; the substitution in the previous question tests one value only and could not rule out disagreement elsewhere.', null, 6, 'auto_fillrect', 15, '1baa6f2c-32e6-5b88-8857-cf5162b6d305'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q21', 'Q21', null, 8, 51.02, 579.87, 544.25, 735.77, 580.28, 811.89, 'Prove', 6, 'Prove that $4(2x + 3) - x = 7x + 12$ for every value of $x$, showing every step and naming the property used at each step.', '$4(2x+3) - x = 8x + 12 - x$ (distributive property) $= 8x + 12 + (-x)$ (definition of subtraction from A.2) $= 8x + (-x) + 12$ (commutative property of addition) $= (8x + (-x)) + 12$ (associative property of addition) $= 7x + 12$ (since $8x - x = (8-1)x = 7x$, the distributive property in reverse).', null, 6, 'auto_fillrect', 16, 'ff52e47a-f78c-582d-baf8-977ff56d7892'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q22', 'Q22', null, 9, 51.02, 103.51, 544.25, 259.42, 580.28, 310.44, 'Determine', 6, 'Two expressions are given: $2(3x + 5) + 4$ and $6x + 14$. Determine whether these expressions are equivalent by proving your conclusion using properties, rather than only substituting a number.', '$2(3x+5) + 4 = 6x + 10 + 4$ (distributive property) $= 6x + (10 + 4)$ (associative property of addition) $= 6x + 14$. The two expressions are therefore equivalent for every value of $x$.', null, 6, 'auto_fillrect', 17, '2406a7e6-58fb-5333-b6a9-a11f7c88c4ed'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q23', 'Q23', null, 9, 51.02, 314.44, 544.25, 470.35, 580.28, 534.84, 'Deduce', 5, 'Deduce, using the distributive property twice, an expression equivalent to $5(x+2) + 3(x+1)$, giving your answer in the form $mx + n$.', '$5(x+2) + 3(x+1) = 5x + 10 + 3x + 3$ (distributive property, applied to each bracket) $= 5x + 3x + 10 + 3$ (commutative and associative properties of addition) $= 8x + 13$. So $m = 8$ and $n = 13$.', null, 5, 'auto_fillrect', 18, 'a43b1011-bef8-5e15-a6fc-3f5b01905664'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q24', 'Q24', 'Part 4', 10, 51.02, 118.45, 544.25, 274.36, 580.28, 354.71, 'Prove', 4, 'A market stall sells notebooks for 3 dollars each and pens for 2 dollars each. A shopper buys $n$ notebooks and $n$ pens (the same number of each). One cashier calculates the total cost as $3n + 2n$. A second cashier calculates it as $n(3 + 2)$. Prove these two totals are equal for every value of $n$, naming the property used.', '$3n + 2n = n(3) + n(2)$ (commutative property of multiplication) $= n(3 + 2)$ (distributive property, read in reverse) $= 5n$. Since only properties true for every number were used, the two cashiers'' totals agree for every value of $n$.', null, 4, 'auto_fillrect', 19, 'd666ceed-0089-5252-950e-ba8bd6e4db48'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q25', 'Q25', null, 10, 51.02, 358.71, 544.25, 514.61, 580.28, 601.58, 'Interpret', 5, 'The stall also charges a fixed 4-dollar bag fee per customer, however many items are bought. A customer buying $n$ notebooks and $n$ pens has total cost $5n + 4$ using the result from the previous question. Interpret what the numbers 5 and 4 each represent in this context, then explain why the fee of 4 dollars cannot be distributed across $n$ the way the 5 dollars was.', 'The 5 is the cost per set of one notebook and one pen (3 + 2 dollars), so it is multiplied by the number of sets $n$. The 4 is the bag fee, charged once per customer, so it does not depend on $n$ at all. The distributive property only applies to a factor multiplying a bracketed sum; the 4 is a separate added term, not a factor of $n$, so there is nothing for it to distribute over. Writing $n(5+4)$ would wrongly charge the bag fee once for every set bought.', null, 5, 'auto_fillrect', 20, '059e5e2c-9df8-5bf5-9ba0-0d6a4da4e022'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q26', 'Q26', null, 10, 51.02, 605.58, 544.25, 761.49, 580.28, 811.89, 'Determine', 6, 'A second stall prices the same combination differently: total cost $= 2(n + 2) + 3n$. Determine whether this second stall''s total cost is equivalent to $5n + 4$ from the previous question, proving your answer using properties.', '$2(n+2) + 3n = 2n + 4 + 3n$ (distributive property) $= 2n + 3n + 4$ (commutative property of addition) $= (2n + 3n) + 4$ (associative property of addition) $= 5n + 4$ (distributive property in reverse). The two totals are equivalent, so a customer pays the same at either stall for every value of $n$.', null, 6, 'auto_fillrect', 21, 'cb1f2cbb-2454-513d-9a2b-ef673d9440ef'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q27', 'Q27', 'Part 5', 11, 51.02, 358.57, 544.25, 514.47, 580.28, 811.89, 'Calculate', 5, 'Marcus is asked to prove $2 \times (3 \times 5) = (2 \times 3) \times (2 \times 5)$. He writes: ''This is true by the distributive property, since 2 is being distributed across the multiplication.'' Calculate both sides to check whether his equation is even numerically true, then justify why his use of the word ''distributive property'' is incorrect, stating what the distributive property actually distributes over.', 'Left side: $2 \times (3 \times 5) = 2 \times 15 = 30$. Right side: $(2 \times 3) \times (2 \times 5) = 6 \times 10 = 60$. Since $30 \neq 60$ the equation is false, so no property could justify it. The distributive property is $a(b+c) = ab+ac$: multiplication distributes over ADDITION (or subtraction), not over multiplication. The correct property for $2 \times (3 \times 5)$ is the associative property of multiplication, which gives $(2 \times 3) \times 5 = 30$ — the 2 is not repeated.', null, 5, 'auto_fillrect', 22, '7a371955-56f9-535d-ac42-dc7695014e27'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q28', 'Q28', null, 12, 51.02, 150.56, 544.25, 306.47, 580.28, 370.96, 'Calculate', 5, 'Marcus is asked to show that $(5 + 2) + 9 = (5 + 9) + 2$. He writes: ''This is true by the associative property, since I just regrouped the numbers.'' Calculate both sides, then justify whether ''associative property'' is the correct name for what Marcus actually did, identifying which property was really used.', 'Left side: $(5+2)+9 = 7+9 = 16$. Right side: $(5+9)+2 = 14+2 = 16$. The equation is true, but the name is wrong. The numbers appear as $5, 2, 9$ on the left and $5, 9, 2$ on the right, so the ORDER of 2 and 9 has changed — that is the commutative property of addition. The associative property alone would only move brackets, giving $5 + (2+9)$ with the order unchanged. A full justification uses both: $(5+2)+9 = 5+(2+9)$ (associative) $= 5+(9+2)$ (commutative) $= (5+9)+2$ (associative).', null, 5, 'auto_fillrect', 23, 'c6fe7b0a-89a1-5055-a2d3-ad2d453f14ef'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q29', 'Q29', 'Part 6', 12, 51.02, 573.94, 544.25, 729.84, 580.28, 811.89, 'Investigate', 3, 'Investigate the statement $(x + y)^2 = x^2 + y^2$ by calculating both sides for $x = 3$, $y = 4$. State whether the statement appears to be true.', 'Left side: $(3+4)^2 = 7^2 = 49$. Right side: $3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25$. Since $49 \neq 25$, the statement is false — squaring a sum is not the same as adding the squares.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 24, '9963869f-395f-5b8d-9ab4-a4f7488b8282'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q30', 'Q30', null, 13, 51.02, 121.51, 544.25, 277.42, 580.28, 342.83, 'Prove', 7, 'Prove that $(x+y)^2 = x^2+2xy+y^2$ for every value of $x$ and $y$, by writing $(x+y)^2$ as $(x+y)(x+y)$ and applying the distributive property twice. Name each property you use.', '$(x+y)^2 = (x+y)(x+y)$ (definition of squaring) $= x(x+y) + y(x+y)$ (distributive property, first application) $= x^2 + xy + yx + y^2$ (distributive property, second application, to each term) $= x^2 + xy + xy + y^2$ (commutative property of multiplication, since $yx = xy$) $= x^2 + 2xy + y^2$ (since $xy + xy = (1+1)xy = 2xy$, the distributive property in reverse). Every step names a property true for all numbers, so the identity holds for every value of $x$ and $y$.', null, 7, 'auto_fillrect', 25, 'bd587fbb-e1a4-5750-9c53-df5db8eccc62'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q31', 'Q31', null, 13, 51.02, 346.83, 544.25, 502.73, 580.28, 575.04, 'Interpret', 5, 'Sketch a square of side length $(x+y)$, divided by one horizontal and one vertical line into four regions of side lengths $x$ and $y$. Interpret each of the four regions'' areas, and explain how their total matches the identity $x^2+2xy+y^2$ proved in the previous question.', 'The square has side $x+y$, so its area is $(x+y)^2$. The two cuts produce four regions: one $x$ by $x$ square of area $x^2$, one $y$ by $y$ square of area $y^2$, and two $x$ by $y$ rectangles each of area $xy$. Their total is $x^2 + xy + xy + y^2 = x^2 + 2xy + y^2$. Since cutting the square does not change its area, $(x+y)^2 = x^2+2xy+y^2$; the coefficient 2 is visible as the two identical rectangles.', null, 5, 'auto_fillrect', 26, '36bb8432-5a49-5b7a-b49d-82774e04394f'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q32', 'Q32', null, 13, 51.02, 579.04, 544.25, 734.95, 580.28, 811.89, 'Determine', 3, 'Determine an expanded expression equivalent to $(x + 3)^2$, using the identity proved in this part.', 'Taking $y = 3$ in $(x+y)^2 = x^2+2xy+y^2$ gives $(x+3)^2 = x^2 + 2(3)x + 3^2 = x^2 + 6x + 9$.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 27, '12321e8e-9c95-5846-851b-9584dc55cd90'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q33', 'Q33', 'Zone (b)', 14, 51.02, 392.93, 544.25, 548.83, 580.28, 599.86, 'Write down', 1, 'A two-pan balance is perfectly level, with unknown weight $x$ grams and a 5-gram weight together on the left pan, and a 20-gram weight on the right pan. Write down the equation that describes this balance.', '$x + 5 = 20$.', null, 1, 'auto_fillrect', 28, 'be033805-95af-55c1-b019-8a2b815eceb8'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q34', 'Q34', null, 14, 51.02, 603.86, 544.25, 759.76, 580.28, 811.89, 'State', 1, 'State what must happen to the right pan of the balance in the previous question if you remove the 5-gram weight from the left pan, in order for the balance to stay level.', '5 grams must also be removed from the right pan.', null, 1, 'auto_fillrect', 29, 'acde69d2-780e-5521-ba8f-20b16e1de134'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q35', 'Q35', null, 15, 51.02, 122.06, 544.25, 277.97, 580.28, 343.65, null, 3, 'Using your answer to the previous question, determine the value of $x$ for the balance $x + 5 = 20$.', 'Removing 5 grams from each pan leaves $x$ on the left and $20 - 5 = 15$ on the right, so $x = 15$ grams.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 30, 'fc9086ca-0290-5ef8-bc0a-f796e317377c'),
  ('997390bb-c0ab-43fe-a123-cfe5f3d80fbe', 'Q36', 'Q36', null, 15, 51.02, 347.65, 544.25, 503.56, 580.28, 811.89, 'Determine', 3, 'Consider the balance $3x = 18$, meaning three identical unknown weights together equal 18 grams. Determine the weight of one unknown, explaining your reasoning in terms of splitting the right pan into three equal groups.', 'The three unknowns are identical, so splitting the 18 grams on the right into three equal groups pairs one group with each unknown. Each group is $18 \div 3 = 6$ grams, so $x = 6$ grams.', null, 3, 'auto_fillrect', 31, '0145bb43-c82c-5caf-b2d3-f539263a6db6');
